787 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105787 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "49c501da-161c-53be-8349-571cc6a07975" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186106746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1045 "The Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186106673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106002 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e8bbfc2d-b5f4-57c0-9027-1b66b844109b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106673 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106673 #edb/ref 1061 20075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106673 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9b7139a7-3f19-5d72-857b-7a01a291e46b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106673 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106674 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106674 #edb/ref 1061 20077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106674 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4921a5cc-f26d-5b7a-9865-e447eec1d53e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106674 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106675 #edb/ref 1061 20078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106675 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "64cdc019-4015-5867-b014-691bcc52330a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106675 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106676 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106676 #edb/ref 1061 20081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106676 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6e9a9912-8def-5a76-892f-0608e119d89c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106676 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106711 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106711 #edb/ref 1061 21410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106711 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5b97f2fc-3025-5372-9dc4-19c05d084b76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106711 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106746 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106746 #edb/ref 1061 23514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106746 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ab53090e-96b8-5dcc-b77f-6ff038689cac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106746 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107090 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a matrix $A,$ an **eigenvector** of $A$ is a *non-zero* vector $\\mathbf{v}$ such that \n\n$$\nA \\mathbf{v} = \\lambda \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nfor some scalar $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ The scalar $\\lambda$ is called the **eigenvalue** corresponding to the eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nFor example, consider the matrix \n$[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ \nand the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ Computing the product $A \\cdot \\mathbf{v},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe've discovered that $A {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}} = {\\color{red}-2} {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.$ Therefore,\n\n- the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ is an *eigenvector* of $A,$ and\n\n- the scalar $\\color{red}\\lambda=-2$ is the *eigenvalue* corresponding to the eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}.$\n\n**Caution:** Remember that an eigenvector must be a *non-zero* vector. This is very important." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107090 #edb/ref 1065 3557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107090 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1014a8e8-38ce-568b-86d7-22f32ef8f697" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107090 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107098 #edb/ref 1064 "Eigenvectors and eigenvalues can be interpreted geometrically.\n\nFirst, recall that if the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is the standard matrix of some linear transformation ![](images/80/80d488fc9d8168cecdf882f1aa178325bb36bd387dd9f86f2b0cd98a5c54f4dc.svg) then the product ![](images/dc/dcc52971e9a7e86ebbe8bf972c562fce13d389f9261b69cba05dcabb1426b9dc.svg) gives the image of ![](images/4f/4fa2300af121b336bb0d7845b8e2b3e34be5a517a1bca45b4ec7b6514b2c544d.svg) under the action of ![](images/a1/a1af41bdb2f2fe410c6f8e02d1a2a0048f0aa470ceef4508753090444ca039be.svg) The image of ![](images/4f/4fa2300af121b336bb0d7845b8e2b3e34be5a517a1bca45b4ec7b6514b2c544d.svg) under the action of ![](images/c1/c15eeb9e7a3ff525fbdcd2f175622bd034f0b34899224f9ce2e08a5300d625de.svg) can appear quite different from the original vector ![](images/92/9221d4dc55c1df8da019956ea23f9d4f9ff3f5b04921ec2158804a31a50e636b.svg)\n\nHowever, for an eigenvector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) with corresponding eigenvalue ![](images/4a/4a2f3891f0b4c8b852767d556e1607028035219b70ab83b4910e95159a930b9c.svg) we get\n\n![](images/ee/eed93a562368727b490c2b63d14f46580dcd0439ac90df23a23f75a31de9e971.svg)\n\nIn other words, when the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) is applied to an eigenvector ![](images/b2/b2766af4a0fe79e81fbd5ac113f0446ce1d111def401abbf09ced1ee62ca1cf1.svg) the image of ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) is a scaled version of the original vector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg), and the scale factor is ![](images/44/44db815dcaf0a9d405026460a655495e055df7e55a785f542f8920ca90af5418.svg)\n\nTo demonstrate, consider once again the following matrix:\n\n![](images/43/43e76f5a5e889ec18a6893be8cdbf4837d3dedfab149a0981ce6619a62718170.svg)\n\nWe've already seen that ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) has an eigenvector ![](images/fe/fef256ade4f52003da67fac5af68ff4b0d4e7f04ef55e72bb0279bdc8f1e8c3c.svg) with corresponding eigenvalue ![](images/b5/b5cdbd2f286044241daca2565a1ab7f70196ecc2cf6503965d9a820faad9b06e.svg) So we have\n\n![](images/2d/2da94bfdde0e3650ded2622748ead5a4c09cabf610f9d181d48ae158d9bd0986.svg)\n\nPlotting the vector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) and its image ![](images/ab/abe8027351c1645f4c582440b8b536f48f3ec35d008570f3432f322fa468e861.svg) in the plane, we get the following picture.\n\n![](images/9a/9aca41e0597b0b1698e8ec24da9966fd9b7e5fee3951081d8abdb0cc0caaac7b.png)\n\nIn general, when we apply a matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) to an eigenvector ![](images/b2/b2766af4a0fe79e81fbd5ac113f0446ce1d111def401abbf09ced1ee62ca1cf1.svg) the resulting vector ![](images/be/befecb9d885ee3bbbb7313401ca3671428e4aced68a4bfdb4b2fc9a005ea2b43.svg) always lies on the same line as ![](images/1f/1f3d1a60926323e963d7563529669e5cf8210771c8cbb0379f7a7d90e7c6aeec.svg)\n\nIf ![](images/8c/8c453358dacec49655b560be52a99066e3cab1d34cfbdbdbaa7bd74d868fef82.svg) then the matrix stretches the vector. For ![](images/ea/ea6d742051f82ff2f258e08ed09c3b3dc6d4c7f9bbbe40fea718aec8183dc44f.svg) the matrix compresses the vector. In addition, a negative eigenvalue ![](images/c9/c91cda6703c5767aaafeca807b07e466f160cb0ade84472ade5fa85d71a5713e.svg) means that ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) is flipped in the opposite direction as well as scaled." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107098 #edb/ref 1065 3963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107098 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cdf6ca75-e37b-552a-a106-ee8ac2f36a61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107098 #edb/ref 1067 "The Geometric Interpretation of Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241333 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241333 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241333 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "11bca0a2-614b-50fd-87dc-eb967675e2fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242038 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242038 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242038 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5f6f56e4-9815-5dda-9f18-707a058e88f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242919 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242919 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242919 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c5428e71-fdc1-5292-bb47-29f43f29145c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243412 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243412 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243412 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fd33897e-31a0-5885-b899-87b6d4853a13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243417 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243417 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243417 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fe3a2465-d2fa-5709-98fd-7d830e35ecb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243448 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243448 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243448 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243448 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186243412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243448 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186243417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243448 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243448 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243448 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186241333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243448 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "05e9ea84-8e38-5ba9-ae83-afa20ad22f9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243987 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243987 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106946" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243987 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243987 #edb/ref 1071 "An eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ is  $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $c.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243987 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dcb8db9c-1fc6-56a1-9d1f-daa26c43ddf5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243987 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] & =3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2c\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1-4c \\\\ -2+8c\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6c\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1-4c=-3 \\\\ -2+8c=6c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n1-4c=-3\\,⟹\\,c=1.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-2+8c=6c\\,⟹\\,c=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321290 #edb/ref 1080 "None" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321290 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321290 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "12b2601b-5328-5b83-9985-c0e63b43e75b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321346 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321346 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321346 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "192c5006-34c0-5b1b-92a9-3b148e013f9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321582 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321582 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321582 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "393d9285-fba2-5766-833d-21bd3ee93339" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321869 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321869 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321869 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5ba02f2e-1ea0-5ac2-bc42-4c65008f940e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322345 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322345 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186322345 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "966970c2-80d2-56c5-8f17-89142d065790" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323494 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323494 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323494 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323494 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186321869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323494 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186322345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323494 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323494 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323494 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186321346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323494 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a7f626c3-961d-5c1d-9776-1c7b61a81852" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323877 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323877 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37090" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323877 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323877 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $5$ then $10v$ is also an eigenvector corresponding to $5.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $6.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323877 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5bcdf208-8bdf-5d43-b755-321074d52b2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323877 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(10v)=10Av=10(5v)=5(10v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(10v)=5(10v),$ and $10v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $10v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=6v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =6.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=4v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342433 #edb/ref 1080 "-6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342433 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342433 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "163f86d2-fdb8-5f21-84f8-737fdc9a2edf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342904 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342904 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342904 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "504eb653-acec-5ae9-a3f1-4c61020829f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343092 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343092 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343092 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "64b87220-b079-5d53-b2c3-64bdc5bb99a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343728 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343728 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186343728 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a94a82b6-33ed-590a-969a-6c33c2ea9547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344161 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344161 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344161 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dabe1242-62e5-5bc0-8259-75a8a4c362bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344603 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186342433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344603 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186342904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344603 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344603 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186343728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344603 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186344161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344603 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344603 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344603 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186343728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186344603 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "43e5d8ce-5e7d-508c-8cea-c2c668a22fc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345108 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186344603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345108 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75610" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345108 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345108 #edb/ref 1071 "An eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}]$ \ncorresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda =5k-4$ is $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3k-1\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345108 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e32f6312-bf7a-5fb4-bf70-bbf6dad31c7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345108 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3k-1\\end{aligned}] & =(5k-4)[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3k-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2+2(3k-1) \\\\ -10+8(3k-1)\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2(5k-4) \\\\ (5k-4)(3k-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6k \\\\ 24k-18\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}10k-8 \\\\ 15{k}^{2}-17k+4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6k=10k-8 \\\\ 24k-18=15{k}^{2}-17k+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n6k=10k-8\\,⟹\\,k=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=2$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24k-18 & =15{k}^{2}-17k+4 \\\\ 24\\cdot 2-18 & =15\\cdot {2}^{2}-17\\cdot 2+4 \\\\ 30 & =30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386229 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186386230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386229 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106886" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386229 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386229 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/59/59bb15a433f4f9299a240fd0c4d03797425994a0aa17d69fa81cf92e6f99cabc.png)\n\nThe vector 